I am not one who knows how to review games, or doesn't do it as properly as one should require it, but I have played this game, both multiplayer and singleplayer a considerable amount and have played the original Mount & Blade to have some ideas as to what I am talking about.
First things first, this game has amazing ideas and a great modding community, able to make great mods raging from the warhammer series (warsword conquest), star wars, or just a complete overhaul of the vanilla game (floris mod pack) or on Europe (Anno Domini 1257). The list goes on and on, but, the same cannot be applied to (most) of the community, especially on Napoleonic Wars, where there are basically only two types of persons who play competitive: Trolls and those who are really serious about it, there is no middle ground.
Secondly, the controls, clunky, and lacking, with only 4 attack directions and 4 blocking ones, it cannot represent fully how battles were fought in both medieval times and the Napoleonic era, speaking of the dlc for the game "Napoleonic Wars"
Grafics are alright, considering this game is from 2009, and I, for one, am able to max it out fully without problems and not using a top pc, but the best feature is the dx7 option, which lets people who have a poor pc play it and not suffer as much lag as playing on dx9, something every game should possess, an option to change which version of dx you would like to play with.
Now, for the campaign part. The open world sandbox is trully amazing, more games should give the player such freedom to make their own path, not just repetitive corridors of non-stop action for 4 hours and the game is done. This games let's YOU be the one who tells the story, with an almost endless campaign, you can be playing this game for about 150 hours just to have the perfect character to whipe out a castle in one clean sweep. What the game gives in liberty of movement it lacks in character to character interaction, being the options to talk to companions, nobles and kings almost the same, almost as if you are talking to a dog, the more they like you, the better the chances there are of they following you and doing tricks in battles (even though they call it feats). Following a major faction can be dull and boring, without any control over were you can attack, you will be stuck trying to help the marshall of the realm pillage 5 farms until he decides to besiege a castle and stop every 5 minutes to go after a band of looters. If you are able to make your own kingdom, you will also find that the enemy does the same thing to you as they do to their other enemies, so make sure you make your farms capable of not being pillaged long enough until you reach the enemy and defeat them in battle.
Speaking of which, the battles, probably 1/3 of the game, it doesn't matter if you put the difficulty on easy or high, the AI will always do the same, charge head-on when they are outnumbered and out matched and make formations when they outnumber the enemy. Sometimes you are lucky to see them on the bottom of a hill, and sitting pretty while you shoot them to death with arrows and do a hammer and anvil with your infantry and cavalry. Apart from that, it's refreshing to see a strategy game where you are the general of your army and you command your troops just like a Caesar or Alexander would have done.
Now, speaking about the generals, we still haven't introduced what you will be doing before you do any of these, your character creation. The game introduces you to the world and it says that noble men are the ones who rule the land and even says for you to play as a lower ranking man or a female because it is more difficult and interesting. I have to tell you that, if you go as a normal man, it's a normal campaign, and it is not different from going as a noble, since every noble in the land does not recognise you, so, basically, you are a low-ranking man with a banner, nuff said. And what about being a woman? Well, now that is a prodigy, it is said that they have a more difficult campaign out of all of the other picks. I am here to tell you that they are wrong, being a woman and commanding men strikes fear into the hearts of other men, you gain renown faster and you are harder to kill because, even if you carry armour fit for a tank, you will be slim and hard to shoot or hit properly with a sword, letting you create armies that make envy to those of kings faster than they can pillage one of those precious farms.
I believe I have covered the basics, so, I'll show the pros and cons and my final veredict:
Pros:
-Great modding community;
-Endless hours of gameplay;
-Freedom in your actions;
-Nicely Built castles and cities, great sieges;
-Epic battles;
-New, fresh perspective on army management and command;
-Character creation and your own path to glory;
-Great Multiplayer games and awesome linebattles, hosted by players;
-Difficulty changes on how you choose to play and play the game;
-Exquisite kingdom management;
-Money management fits 13th century Europe, loot and sell;
-Tourneys are fun and you earn a great profit (Even though they are not 100% realistic).
Cons:
-Dreadful online community (advise to play with friends for full fun);
-Lacking melle options;
-Horrible AI, both in battles and on the campaign map;
-Easy AI in battles;
-Boring AI in conversations, factor determinded by how they like you (persuation does almost nothing);
-Females get really easy campaign.
Final veredict: 8/10
This game is not perfect, but, when you understand what you are acctually doing in the world, it gets real fun, really quick, so, I trully advise playing this game.

I finished the game and after some time reflecting this is my review:
Pros:
- Gameplay was fun overall;
- Nice graphics;
- Hunting was intense, fun and rewarding (without potions);
- If you are the type of player that likes to play stealth  you'll get rewarded (with extra XP);
- The characters were in "place", they represent well what they were suppose to do;
- Charismatic Villain ( Vaas);
- Some fun side quests;
- Large area to explore;
- Treasure/ cards hunting ( they reward you with gold, lore and special weapons);
- Mini puzzles to get to the top of the towers;
- Nice Co-op;
- Took me 25 hours to complete on normal and i didn't collect all the stuff and neither unlocked all the outpost/ towers.
Cons:
- Boring protagonist, pretty clichê (if you ask me);
- The characters speak in combat therefore you'll miss what they are saying because you're focusing on the combat and you can't even "replay" or have a "quest jornal";
- The story is confusing, and doesn't give much answers ( there is a interesting "Symmetry Theory" on youtube that can help you out), but still we dont know if thats the truth;
- You can get all the upgrates from hunting on the first island;
- After Vaas died the story became pretty boring and a "standard shooting game".
- Hoyt was pretty hyped at the beggining of the game but we saw pretty much nothing of him;
- On the second island you can just fast travel next to the main missions without unlocking any outpost/tower;
- The missions were harder as the game goes on but even if you died you came back with the progress that you had unlocked, like that mission that you and Sam had to destroy 5 fuel depots, you destroyed 2 and died and came back with 2 already destroyed instead of totaly restarted;
- Uplay can be a pain for some players;
I  give it an 8,5/10
I liked the experience of the game and I think you wont regreat buying it.

